#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/new-years.tsx:34
msgid "The month of January is named after Janus, the Roman god of doorways and beginnings, traditionally depicted as facing both the past and the future."
msgstr "The month of January is named after Janus, the Roman god of doorways and beginnings, traditionally depicted as facing both the past and the future."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/new-years.tsx:51
msgid "The new year doesn't arrive all at once — it rolls across the planet over the course of a full day, time zone by time zone."
msgstr "The new year doesn't arrive all at once — it rolls across the planet over the course of a full day, time zone by time zone."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/leonids.tsx:108
msgid "The next potential Leonid storm window is in the 2030s, when Earth is expected to pass through a particularly dense ribbon of Tempel–Tuttle debris."
msgstr "The next potential Leonid storm window is in the 2030s, when Earth is expected to pass through a particularly dense ribbon of Tempel–Tuttle debris."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/holi.tsx:59
msgid "The night before Holi features Holika Dahan, a ceremonial bonfire that symbolises the passing of winter and the renewal of life."
msgstr "The night before Holi features Holika Dahan, a ceremonial bonfire that symbolises the passing of winter and the renewal of life."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/perseids.tsx:108
msgid "The number of meteors you see depends on your location, the darkness and clarity of the sky, and the shower's activity that year. A high radiant and a dark sky usually offer better viewing."
msgstr "The number of meteors you see depends on your location, the darkness and clarity of the sky, and the shower's activity that year. A high radiant and a dark sky usually offer better viewing."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/spring-equinox.tsx:88
msgid "The old tradition of balancing an egg on its end at the equinox is a myth (you can do it any day of the year), but people keep trying anyway. It's become its own kind of ritual."
msgstr "The old tradition of balancing an egg on its end at the equinox is a myth (you can do it any day of the year), but people keep trying anyway. It's become its own kind of ritual."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/valentines.tsx:65
msgid "The oldest known Valentine is a fifteenth-century poem, written from a cell in the Tower of London — a love letter composed under the worst possible circumstances."
msgstr "The oldest known Valentine is a fifteenth-century poem, written from a cell in the Tower of London — a love letter composed under the worst possible circumstances."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/black-hole.tsx:76
msgid "The original image was not a conventional photograph. It was computed from synchronized radio data recorded by observatories around Earth."
msgstr "The original image was not a conventional photograph. It was computed from synchronized radio data recorded by observatories around Earth."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/orionids.tsx:52
msgid "The Orionids are an annual meteor shower formed from debris left by Halley’s Comet, reaching their peak in October."
msgstr "The Orionids are an annual meteor shower formed from debris left by Halley’s Comet, reaching their peak in October."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/orionids.tsx:68
msgid "The Orionids are visible from both hemispheres and are known for producing bright, fast-moving meteors."
msgstr "The Orionids are visible from both hemispheres and are known for producing bright, fast-moving meteors."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/perseids.tsx:51
msgid "The Perseids are a bright annual meteor shower formed from debris left by Comet Swift–Tuttle."
msgstr "The Perseids are a bright annual meteor shower formed from debris left by Comet Swift–Tuttle."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/perseids.tsx:68
msgid "The Perseids are sometimes known as the Tears of Saint Lawrence, as their peak often falls near the feast day of Saint Lawrence in mid-August."
msgstr "The Perseids are sometimes known as the Tears of Saint Lawrence, as their peak often falls near the feast day of Saint Lawrence in mid-August."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/total-solar-eclipse.tsx:37
msgid "The phase of totality is short and can only be seen from a narrow path across the Earth’s surface."
msgstr "The phase of totality is short and can only be seen from a narrow path across the Earth’s surface."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/quadrantids.tsx:106
msgid "The Quadrantid peak is one of the narrowest of any major shower, sometimes lasting only six hours. Catching it is partly a matter of luck and geography."
msgstr "The Quadrantid peak is one of the narrowest of any major shower, sometimes lasting only six hours. Catching it is partly a matter of luck and geography."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/quadrantids.tsx:52
msgid "The Quadrantids are a meteor shower that peaks in early January and is known for being brief yet often intense."
msgstr "The Quadrantids are a meteor shower that peaks in early January and is known for being brief yet often intense."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/meteor-showers/viewing-guide.tsx:188
msgid "The radiant is where the trails appear to come from. Meteors can cross any part of the sky: look roughly halfway up, away from the Moon, with a wide, unobstructed view."
msgstr "The radiant is where the trails appear to come from. Meteors can cross any part of the sky: look roughly halfway up, away from the Moon, with a wide, unobstructed view."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/lunar-new-year.tsx:83
msgid "The season builds toward the Lantern Festival on the fifteenth night, when thousands of lanterns are released into the sky."
msgstr "The season builds toward the Lantern Festival on the fifteenth night, when thousands of lanterns are released into the sky."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/lyrids.tsx:73
msgid "The shower originates from Comet Thatcher, which returns to the inner solar system roughly every four hundred and fifteen years."
msgstr "The shower originates from Comet Thatcher, which returns to the inner solar system roughly every four hundred and fifteen years."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/quadrantids.tsx:68
msgid "The shower takes its name from Quadrans Muralis, a former constellation that no longer appears on modern star charts."
msgstr "The shower takes its name from Quadrans Muralis, a former constellation that no longer appears on modern star charts."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/eta-aquariids.tsx:74
msgid "The shower’s radiant lies near the constellation Aquarius, rising before dawn when viewing conditions are at their best."
msgstr "The shower’s radiant lies near the constellation Aquarius, rising before dawn when viewing conditions are at their best."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/meteor-showers/viewing-guide.tsx:256
msgid "The sky stays too bright for a useful viewing window at this location on this night."
msgstr "The sky stays too bright for a useful viewing window at this location on this night."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/spring-equinox.tsx:55
msgid "The spring equinox marks the moment when day and night stand in near-perfect balance."
msgstr "The spring equinox marks the moment when day and night stand in near-perfect balance."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/quadrantids.tsx:84
msgid "The stream of debris that creates the Quadrantids is unusually narrow, so Earth passes through it quickly."
msgstr "The stream of debris that creates the Quadrantids is unusually narrow, so Earth passes through it quickly."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/summer-solstice.tsx:55
msgid "The summer solstice marks the longest day of the year, when the sun reaches its highest path across the sky."
msgstr "The summer solstice marks the longest day of the year, when the sun reaches its highest path across the sky."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/black-hole.tsx:82
msgid "The target was M87*, a black hole with a mass of billions of Suns, located about 55 million light-years away."
msgstr "The target was M87*, a black hole with a mass of billions of Suns, located about 55 million light-years away."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/day-of-the-dead.tsx:33
msgid "The tradition grows from Indigenous Mexican beliefs about death and the afterlife, later blending with Catholic observances of All Saints’ and All Souls’ Days."
msgstr "The tradition grows from Indigenous Mexican beliefs about death and the afterlife, later blending with Catholic observances of All Saints’ and All Souls’ Days."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/christmas.tsx:70
msgid "The tradition of a decorated Christmas tree only became widespread in the English-speaking world after an 1848 illustration of Queen Victoria and Prince Albert’s tree was published — and went viral, by Victorian standards."
msgstr "The tradition of a decorated Christmas tree only became widespread in the English-speaking world after an 1848 illustration of Queen Victoria and Prince Albert’s tree was published — and went viral, by Victorian standards."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/halloween.tsx:72
msgid "The tradition of trick-or-treating in its modern form only became widespread in the 1950s, though the custom of going door to door in disguise is centuries older."
msgstr "The tradition of trick-or-treating in its modern form only became widespread in the 1950s, though the custom of going door to door in disguise is centuries older."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/winter-solstice.tsx:55
msgid "The winter solstice marks the shortest day and longest night of the year, and the turning point toward gradually longer daylight."
msgstr "The winter solstice marks the shortest day and longest night of the year, and the turning point toward gradually longer daylight."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/lunar-new-year.tsx:73
msgid "The zodiac animal associated with the year is believed to shape its character and fortunes."
msgstr "The zodiac animal associated with the year is believed to shape its character and fortunes."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/quadrantids.tsx:100
msgid "Their likely parent body, asteroid 2003 EH1, may be a dead comet — one that exhausted its volatile ices and now orbits as a dark, quiet rock."
msgstr "Their likely parent body, asteroid 2003 EH1, may be a dead comet — one that exhausted its volatile ices and now orbits as a dark, quiet rock."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/orionids.tsx:58
msgid "Their meteors appear to radiate from the region near the constellation Orion, a figure long woven into myth and storytelling."
msgstr "Their meteors appear to radiate from the region near the constellation Orion, a figure long woven into myth and storytelling."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/orionids.tsx:74
msgid "Their peak often coincides with long, dark viewing hours, when observing conditions are especially favourable."
msgstr "Their peak often coincides with long, dark viewing hours, when observing conditions are especially favourable."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/events/perseids.tsx:88
#~ msgid "Their radiant lies in the constellation Perseus, and the shower is especially prominent in the northern hemisphere, though visible worldwide."
#~ msgstr "Their radiant lies in the constellation Perseus, and the shower is especially prominent in the northern hemisphere, though visible worldwide."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/perseids.tsx:57
msgid "Their radiant lies in the constellation Perseus. The shower is best placed in the northern hemisphere, with visibility decreasing farther south."
msgstr "Their radiant lies in the constellation Perseus. The shower is best placed in the northern hemisphere, with visibility decreasing farther south."

#. placeholder {0}: Math.round(rain)
#: src/features/weather/ui/tile.tsx:567
msgid "There is a {0}% chance of precipitation."
msgstr "There is a {0}% chance of precipitation."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/easter.tsx:51
msgid "There's a reason the holiday lands in spring — it borrows heavily from the season's own sense of things starting over."
msgstr "There's a reason the holiday lands in spring — it borrows heavily from the season's own sense of things starting over."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/leonids.tsx:75
msgid "These events played an important role in the development of scientific understanding of meteor showers."
msgstr "These events played an important role in the development of scientific understanding of meteor showers."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/geminids.tsx:58
msgid "They are especially known for their steady rates and vivid, often colourful trails."
msgstr "They are especially known for their steady rates and vivid, often colourful trails."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/lyrids.tsx:58
msgid "They are typically a gentle shower that rewards patient skywatching."
msgstr "They are typically a gentle shower that rewards patient skywatching."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/eta-aquariids.tsx:58
msgid "They peak in early May and are especially well seen from the southern hemisphere, though northern skies still catch their share of streaks."
msgstr "They peak in early May and are especially well seen from the southern hemisphere, though northern skies still catch their share of streaks."

#: src/features/settings/ui/sections/integrations-settings.tsx:135
msgid "This account's sign-in expired. Please reconnect it."
msgstr "This account's sign-in expired. Please reconnect it."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/quadrantids.tsx:90
msgid "This makes the period of strongest activity short, but it can be especially spectacular."
msgstr "This makes the period of strongest activity short, but it can be especially spectacular."

#: src/features/seasonal-events/definitions/black-hole.tsx:59
